{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>His eyes are dark (חַכְלִילִי עֵינַיִם)</b>. The commentators (Rashi, Ibn Ezra, and Radak) said that this is a matter of redness — that his eyes grow red from excessive wine drinking, similar to \"to whom is redness of eyes (לְמִי חַכְלִילוּת עֵינָיִם)\" (Proverbs 23:29). But what seems right in my eyes is that it is the opposite of \"you painted (כָּחַלְתְּ) your eyes\" (Ezekiel 23:40), and the lamed is doubled in it according to the practice of many words, and its meaning is like the eye-paint (כָּחֹל) that is well known and common in the words of our Sages (Kelim 13:2). So too is its name in Arabic: \"al-kuḥul.\" It means that he paints his eyes with wine, for just as others paint them with stibium (פּוּךְ), which is the \"al-kuḥul,\" so he paints them with wine; and just as others whiten their teeth with cosmetic polishes, so he whitens them with milk. And the metaphor is for the abundance of wine and milk in his land, as Onkelos mentioned (Onkelos on Genesis 49:12). And likewise, \"to whom is redness of eyes (לְמִי חַכְלִילוּת עֵינָיִם)\" — like כַּחְלִילוּת — means that his eyes are painted (darkened) from wine and he cannot hide his drunkenness. Or, \"to whom is redness of eyes (לְמִי חַכְלִילוּת עֵינָיִם)\" may mean that he constantly needs to apply eye-paint (לִכְחוֹל) to his eyes — those who linger over wine — for wine darkens their eyes and brings tears, and their [eyes'] sockets waste away, so that his eyes must always have eye-paint (מִכְחוֹל). Scripture recounts the disgrace of wine in the evils that come upon a man from outside — in quarrels and wounds — and that \"woe and alas\" will be heard in his house, and it mentions the harm that befalls his body from it: the dimming of his eyes and their many diseases. And this is a correct interpretation and meaning:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:00.271858482Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}